\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Analysable windows per participant and class after the containment, transition-guard and startup-guard exclusions. Participants are labelled P1--P5 here and in every later figure, in the same order as Table~\ref {tab:persubject}. Class sizes are strongly unequal both between classes---chewing supplies 58.9\% of all windows---and between participants within a class, which is why class weighting, minority-only augmentation and participant-level aggregation are all used. Section~\ref {sec:constraints} shows that the between-participant inequality is a consequence of how differently participants marked the trigger.}}{7}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sample_flow}{{2}{7}{Analysable windows per participant and class after the containment, transition-guard and startup-guard exclusions. Participants are labelled P1--P5 here and in every later figure, in the same order as Table~\ref {tab:persubject}. Class sizes are strongly unequal both between classes---chewing supplies 58.9\% of all windows---and between participants within a class, which is why class weighting, minority-only augmentation and participant-level aggregation are all used. Section~\ref {sec:constraints} shows that the between-participant inequality is a consequence of how differently participants marked the trigger}{figure.2}{}}
