\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces RQ1. The EMG filter chain is the only thing that varies: the same 6,173 windows, the same five outer-fold assignments and the same labels in every arm, verified window by window across the two prediction ledgers. Residual mains power is the share of 20--450-Hz power within $\pm 3$~Hz of a mains multiple, averaged over participant $\times $ class cells; a perfectly white signal would score 0.098. ``Inv.'' counts participant pairs in which one person's resting EMG exceeded another person's active EMG. Differences quoted in the text are computed before rounding, so they may differ by 0.1 from the difference of the rounded values shown here.}}{13}{table.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:filter}{{3}{13}{RQ1. The EMG filter chain is the only thing that varies: the same 6,173 windows, the same five outer-fold assignments and the same labels in every arm, verified window by window across the two prediction ledgers. Residual mains power is the share of 20--450-Hz power within $\pm 3$~Hz of a mains multiple, averaged over participant $\times $ class cells; a perfectly white signal would score 0.098. ``Inv.'' counts participant pairs in which one person's resting EMG exceeded another person's active EMG. Differences quoted in the text are computed before rounding, so they may differ by 0.1 from the difference of the rounded values shown here}{table.3}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Five-class performance on the corrected signal, averaged over the five held-out participants and three seeds. Classes are rest, movement, clenching, grinding, and chewing. The two baseline rows come from the screening harness of Section~\ref {sec:screening} on the identical windows and folds, and are optimistic in level, which makes the network's margin over them a conservative estimate. Values describe this five-participant dataset and are not population-level superiority tests.}}{13}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:results}{{4}{13}{Five-class performance on the corrected signal, averaged over the five held-out participants and three seeds. Classes are rest, movement, clenching, grinding, and chewing. The two baseline rows come from the screening harness of Section~\ref {sec:screening} on the identical windows and folds, and are optimistic in level, which makes the network's margin over them a conservative estimate. Values describe this five-participant dataset and are not population-level superiority tests}{table.4}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The mains-harmonic defect, expressed in the terms a classification paper is read in. (a) Residual mains power for every candidate chain that was implemented and measured, against the 0.098 a white signal would score; the chain used is the lowest, and was chosen on this axis rather than on classification score. (b) Screening macro-F1 per held-out participant before and after the correction, on the 28 EMG features and identical folds. (c) The mechanism: each activity's median RMS divided by that participant's own resting RMS, five participants per condition, before (down triangles) and after (up triangles). (d) Between-participant amplitude spread, and the number of participant pairs in which one person's resting EMG exceeded another person's active EMG. No panel of this figure reads the microphone.}}{14}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:measurement_chain}{{6}{14}{The mains-harmonic defect, expressed in the terms a classification paper is read in. (a) Residual mains power for every candidate chain that was implemented and measured, against the 0.098 a white signal would score; the chain used is the lowest, and was chosen on this axis rather than on classification score. (b) Screening macro-F1 per held-out participant before and after the correction, on the 28 EMG features and identical folds. (c) The mechanism: each activity's median RMS divided by that participant's own resting RMS, five participants per condition, before (down triangles) and after (up triangles). (d) Between-participant amplitude spread, and the number of participant pairs in which one person's resting EMG exceeded another person's active EMG. No panel of this figure reads the microphone}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Per-class performance on held-out windows, averaged over three seeds. AUC is one-vs-rest, computed from held-out probabilities.}}{14}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:perclass}{{5}{14}{Per-class performance on held-out windows, averaged over three seeds. AUC is one-vs-rest, computed from held-out probabilities}{table.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces One-vs-rest ROC (left) and precision--recall (right) curves for the five experimental classes, pooled over the held-out windows of seed 0 (the lowest seed index, a fixed rule rather than a choice made after seeing the scores). Each curve depicts one trained model per fold, so the legend values are that seed's; the three-seed means are in Table~\ref {tab:perclass}. Precision--recall separates the classes far more sharply than ROC does, because it is sensitive to the very unequal class sizes.}}{15}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:roc_pr}{{7}{15}{One-vs-rest ROC (left) and precision--recall (right) curves for the five experimental classes, pooled over the held-out windows of seed 0 (the lowest seed index, a fixed rule rather than a choice made after seeing the scores). Each curve depicts one trained model per fold, so the legend values are that seed's; the three-seed means are in Table~\ref {tab:perclass}. Precision--recall separates the classes far more sharply than ROC does, because it is sensitive to the very unequal class sizes}{figure.7}{}}
